    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_adc4f4875982f4b431b142daefa7c792d3d725290d48be9f02e00ae43ff2db39",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "At c=1 and c=2, exceptional Lambda and Eisenstein poles change the required subtraction, constant or logarithmic terms, and contour bookkeeping, but the main architecture survives through pole-specific repairs. The c=1 functional is regularized by a Mellin zero that cancels the apparent zeta(1) pole, while at c=2 regularized Eisenstein subtraction enables scalar projection, transformed-zeta-zero residues, and evaluation of the arithmetic remainder with the same hypergeometric kernel form.",
